+# Deploying Fizzy with Kamal
+
+The root `config/deploy.yml` deploys self-hosted Fizzy to **one Linux server**. The app container runs Rails, SQLite, and Solid Queue background workers (including recurring jobs). A separate Kamal Proxy container terminates HTTPS with an automatically renewed Let's Encrypt certificate. No MySQL, Redis, S3, or separate job server is needed.
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- Fork and clone this repository. Install the Ruby version in `.ruby-version` and run `bundle install` on your deployment machine.
+- Install and start Docker on your deployment machine, where Kamal builds the image.
+- Have a Linux server with Docker installed and SSH access as `root` (Kamal's default). For another user, add `ssh: { user: your-user }` to the config; that user must be able to run Docker.
+- Point your hostname's DNS A record (and AAAA record, if used) directly at the server. Allow inbound TCP ports 80 and 443 for HTTPS and certificate issuance, and your SSH port for deployment. Do not expose the app container or its SQLite databases directly.
+- Have a container registry account and an SMTP server that can send mail from your chosen address. Production sign-in uses email verification codes, so working email delivery is required.
+
+Use the open-source bundle, not the private SaaS deployment: if you enabled SaaS mode locally, run deployment commands with `SAAS=false BUNDLE_GEMFILE=Gemfile`.
+
+## Configure your fork
+
+Edit `config/deploy.yml`:
+
+1. Replace `fizzy.example.com` in `servers.web` with your hostname, without `https://` or a path. The YAML anchor reuses it for proxy SSL, application URLs, and the SMTP domain.
+2. Replace `your-github-user` in `image` and `registry.username` with your GitHub username (use a lowercase image path). The example uses GHCR; you can change `registry.server`, `image`, and `registry.username` for another registry. For GHCR, use a token with package read/write permissions, authorized for your organization if necessary.
+3. Set `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, and `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` for your mail provider. The default is authenticated SMTP with STARTTLS on port 587. For implicit TLS on port 465, set `SMTP_PORT: 465`, `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO: "false"`, and add `SMTP_TLS: "true"` under `env.clear`. For an unauthenticated relay, leave the SMTP username and password empty.
+4. Set `builder.arch` to match the server (`amd64` or `arm64`). The default limits the app to two web workers and one job worker process; adjust `WEB_CONCURRENCY` and `JOB_CONCURRENCY` for your server's resources.
+
+The Dockerfile serves HTTP on port 80 through Thruster. Leave `proxy.app_port: 80`, `proxy.ssl: true`, `ASSUME_SSL: "true"`, and `FORCE_SSL: "true"` in place. Do not enable TLS inside the app container.
+
+## Supply secrets
+
+Generate a secret once with `bin/rails secret`. Create a local `.env` file:
+
+```sh
+KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD='your-registry-access-token'
+SECRET_KEY_BASE='paste-the-generated-secret-here'
+SMTP_USER_NAME='your-smtp-username'
+SMTP_PASSWORD='your-smtp-password'
+```
+
+Keep `SECRET_KEY_BASE` stable across deploys. Fizzy uses it to sign sessions and other tokens; replacing it invalidates them. This setup uses `SECRET_KEY_BASE` directly and does not require Rails encrypted credentials or a `RAILS_MASTER_KEY`.
+
+`.env` is ignored by Git and Docker. Never commit secrets to `config/deploy.yml` or `.kamal/secrets`. The committed `.kamal/secrets` only references environment variables. Protect `.env` with `chmod 600 .env`, then load it into your shell before each Kamal command:
+
+```sh
+set -a
+. ./.env
+set +a
+```
+
+Alternatively, export the four variables from your password manager. SMTP credentials are sent to the app as secrets; the registry token is used only by Kamal.
+
+## Deploy
+
+Commit your configuration changes so Kamal builds the configured revision, then run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+(`bundle exec kamal setup` is equivalent.) Kamal builds and pushes the image, starts the proxy, and starts Fizzy. The container entrypoint runs `db:prepare` before starting the web server, creating or migrating all four SQLite databases. Solid Queue starts alongside Puma and processes jobs on this same box.
+
+Visit `https://your-hostname`, sign up, and check that your verification email arrives. If setup fails, check DNS, firewall rules, registry credentials, and `bin/kamal logs`.
+
+For later releases, commit or pull your changes, reload your secrets, and run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+Useful commands:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal logs
+bin/kamal console
+bin/kamal shell
+bin/kamal app exec --reuse 'bin/rails db:migrate:status'
+```
+
+## Persistent data and backups
+
+The named Docker volume `fizzy_storage` is mounted at `/rails/storage`. It contains:
+
+- `production.sqlite3`: application data
+- `production_queue.sqlite3`: background jobs and recurring-job state
+- `production_cache.sqlite3` and `production_cable.sqlite3`: cache and realtime messaging
+- `production/files`: uploaded attachments
+
+New containers reuse this volume on every deploy. Do not rename it, delete it, or run `docker volume prune` while it is unused. This is a single-server setup: moving to another server requires transferring the volume as well as the app configuration and secrets.
+
+Persistence is not a backup. Back up the volume to another machine regularly and test restoring it. For a simple consistent backup, stop Fizzy with `bin/kamal app stop`, back up the **entire** volume (including SQLite WAL files and attachments), then restart it with `bin/kamal app start`. For online backups, use SQLite's backup API rather than copying live database files. A code rollback does not roll back database migrations; keep backups before upgrades.
+
+Browser push notifications are optional. To enable them, generate VAPID keys as described in the [development guide](development.md#web-push-notifications), add `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` and `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` to `env.secret` and `.kamal/secrets` as environment references, and export their values alongside your other secrets.
diff --git a/test/lib/deployment_configuration_test.rb b/test/lib/deployment_configuration_test.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4605b9d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/lib/deployment_configuration_test.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@
+require "test_helper"
+require "kamal"
+
+class DeploymentConfigurationTest < ActiveSupport::TestCase
+  test "Kamal deploys one web container with proxy SSL and local jobs" do
+    deployment = deployment_configuration
+
+    assert_equal [ "web" ], deployment.roles.map(&:name)
+    assert_equal 1, deployment.app_hosts.size
+    assert_empty deployment.accessories
+    assert_equal true, deployment.raw_config.proxy["ssl"]
+    assert_equal 80, deployment.raw_config.proxy["app_port"]
+    assert_equal deployment.primary_host, deployment.raw_config.proxy["host"]
+    assert_equal deployment.primary_host, deployment.env.clear["APP_FQDN"]
+    assert_equal "true", deployment.env.clear["SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA"]
+    assert_equal "true", deployment.env.clear["ASSUME_SSL"]
+    assert_equal "true", deployment.env.clear["FORCE_SSL"]
+  end
+
+  test "SQLite databases and local uploads use persistent storage" do
+    deployment = deployment_configuration
+
+    assert_equal "sqlite", deployment.env.clear["DATABASE_ADAPTER"]
+    assert_equal "local", deployment.env.clear["ACTIVE_STORAGE_SERVICE"]
+    assert_includes deployment.raw_config.volumes, "fizzy_storage:/rails/storage"
+    assert_equal "/rails/public/assets", deployment.raw_config.asset_path
+
+    databases = YAML.load_file(Rails.root.join("config/database.sqlite.yml"), aliases: true).fetch("production")
+    assert_equal %w[ primary cable cache queue ], databases.keys
+    databases.each_value { |database| assert_match %r{\Astorage/}, database.fetch("database") }
+
+    storage = YAML.safe_load(ERB.new(File.read(Rails.root.join("config/storage.oss.yml"))).result)
+    assert_equal "Disk", storage.fetch("local").fetch("service")
+    assert_match %r{\A#{Regexp.escape(Rails.root.join("storage").to_s)}/}, storage.fetch("local").fetch("root")
+  end
+
+  test "deployment secrets come from the environment and not clear config" do
+    secret_names = %w[ KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD SECRET_KEY_BASE SMTP_USER_NAME SMTP_PASSWORD ]
+    original_environment = ENV.to_h
+    secret_names.each { |name| ENV[name] = "test-#{name.downcase}" }
+
+    deployment = deployment_configuration
+    assert_equal ENV["KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD"], deployment.registry.password
+    assert_equal secret_names.drop(1), deployment.env.secret_keys
+    secret_names.drop(1).each do |name|
+      assert_equal ENV[name], deployment.env.to_h[name]
+      assert_not deployment.env.clear.key?(name)
+    end
+  ensure
+    ENV.replace(original_environment)
+  end
+
+  private
+    def deployment_configuration
+      Kamal::Configuration.create_from(config_file: Rails.root.join("config/deploy.yml"), version: "deployment-test")
+    end
+end
diff --git a/test/lib/production_configuration_test.rb b/test/lib/production_configuration_test.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..d873bc6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/lib/production_configuration_test.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,107 @@
+require "test_helper"
+
+class ProductionConfigurationTest < ActiveSupport::TestCase
+  ENVIRONMENT_KEYS = %w[ APP_FQDN SMTP_ADDRESS SMTP_PORT SMTP_DOMAIN SMTP_USER_NAME SMTP_PASSWORD SMTP_AUTHENTICATION SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO SMTP_TLS ]
+
+  setup do
+    Fizzy.stubs(:saas?).returns(false)
+  end
+
+  test "sets HTTPS URLs for mailers and background jobs" do
+    application = production_application("APP_FQDN" => "fizzy.example.com")
+    expected = { host: "fizzy.example.com", protocol: "https" }
+
+    assert_equal expected, application.config.action_mailer.default_url_options
+    assert_equal expected, application.config.action_controller.default_url_options
+    assert_equal expected, application.routes.default_url_options
+  end
+
+  test "configures authenticated SMTP with STARTTLS by default" do
+    application = production_application(
+      "APP_FQDN" => "fizzy.example.com", "SMTP_ADDRESS" => "smtp.example.com",
+      "SMTP_USER_NAME" => "smtp-user", "SMTP_PASSWORD" => "smtp-password"
+    )
+
+    assert_equal :smtp, application.config.action_mailer.delivery_method
+    assert_equal({
+      address: "smtp.example.com", port: 587, domain: "fizzy.example.com",
+      user_name: "smtp-user", password: "smtp-password", authentication: :plain,
+      enable_starttls_auto: true, tls: false
+    }, application.config.action_mailer.smtp_settings)
+  end
+
+  test "allows a relay without authentication and a custom SMTP domain" do
+    application = production_application(
+      "SMTP_ADDRESS" => "relay.example.com", "SMTP_PORT" => "25", "SMTP_DOMAIN" => "example.com",
+      "SMTP_USER_NAME" => "", "SMTP_PASSWORD" => "", "SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO" => "false"
+    )
+    settings = application.config.action_mailer.smtp_settings
+
+    assert_equal 25, settings[:port]
+    assert_equal "example.com", settings[:domain]
+    assert_nil settings[:user_name]
+    assert_nil settings[:password]
+    assert_equal false, settings[:enable_starttls_auto]
+  end
+
+  test "supports implicit TLS and another authentication mechanism" do
+    application = production_application(
+      "SMTP_ADDRESS" => "smtp.example.com", "SMTP_PORT" => "465", "SMTP_AUTHENTICATION" => "login",
+      "SMTP_TLS" => "true", "SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO" => "false"
+    )
+    settings = application.config.action_mailer.smtp_settings
+
+    assert_equal 465, settings[:port]
+    assert_equal :login, settings[:authentication]
+    assert_equal true, settings[:tls]
+    assert_equal false, settings[:enable_starttls_auto]
+  end
+
+  test "does not require hostname or SMTP configuration to build assets" do
+    application = production_application
+
+    assert_nil application.config.action_mailer.default_url_options
+    assert_nil application.config.action_mailer.smtp_settings
+    assert_equal :solid_queue, application.config.active_job.queue_adapter
+    assert_equal :local, application.config.active_storage.service
+  end
+
+  test "does not override SaaS email configuration" do
+    Fizzy.stubs(:saas?).returns(true)
+    application = production_application("APP_FQDN" => "fizzy.example.com", "SMTP_ADDRESS" => "smtp.example.com") do |app|
+      app.config.action_mailer.default_url_options = { host: "app.fizzy.do", protocol: "https" }
+      app.config.action_mailer.smtp_settings = { address: "smtp-outbound", port: 25 }
+    end
+
+    assert_equal "app.fizzy.do", application.config.action_mailer.default_url_options[:host]
+    assert_equal({ address: "smtp-outbound", port: 25 }, application.config.action_mailer.smtp_settings)
+  end
+
+  test "rejects invalid SMTP ports" do
+    assert_raises(ArgumentError) { production_application("SMTP_ADDRESS" => "smtp.example.com", "SMTP_PORT" => "invalid") }
+  end
+
+  private
+    def production_application(environment = {})
+      original_environment = ENV.to_h
+      ENVIRONMENT_KEYS.each { |key| ENV.delete(key) }
+      ENV.update(environment)
+
+      config_path = Rails.root.join("config/environments/production.rb")
+      application = Class.new(Rails::Application).new
+      # Railtie options are shared by application classes; isolate these from the test app.
+      %i[ public_file_server active_storage action_controller action_mailer active_job solid_queue i18n active_support active_record ].each do |name|
+        application.config.public_send("#{name}=", application.config.public_send(name).deep_dup)
+      end
+      application.config.action_mailer.default_url_options = nil
+      application.config.action_mailer.smtp_settings = nil
+      application.config.active_storage.service = nil
+      yield application if block_given?
+      Rails.stubs(:application).returns(application)
+      load config_path
+      application
+    ensure
+      Rails.unstub(:application)
+      ENV.replace(original_environment)
+    end
+end
